{"text":"Recent work supports learning temporal relevance rather than assuming older interactions are obsolete. [TLSTSRec (online 2024; volume 2025)](https://doi.org/10.3233/IDA-240051) combines long- and short-term temporal information through adaptive fusion layers that personalize their relative contributions. A [2026 BERT4Rec session-enhancement paper](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s44163-026-01554-z) jointly trains a bidirectional long-history encoder, time-aware short-session attention, and gated representation fusion. These provide structural precedents, but their reported gains do not establish improvement under the pinned RecHarness protocol.

The clearest relative-time mechanism remains [TiSASRec (2020)](https://cseweb.ucsd.edu/~jmcauley/pdfs/wsdm20b.pdf): pairwise interaction intervals, personalized scaling, clipping, and learned interval embeddings inside attention. Its temporal mechanism could inspire a BERT4Rec adaptation without adopting its causal backbone. Proposed experiment: keep terminal-mask training fixed; separately add learned, bucketed time-gap attention biases and a content-conditioned recency gate within the same jointly trained network. Preserve a full-history residual path so useful older interests remain accessible; avoid mandatory monotonic decay. This adaptation is a hypothesis, not an established result.

Compare terminal-mask-only, relative-time-only, gate-only, and combined variants using macro validation HR@10 across all four categories. Retain BERT4Rec’s [masked-target objective](https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.06690), appended-mask inference, and final nonpadding readout. Use only observed historical timestamps; the unknown next interaction’s timestamp must not enter the appended-mask features. Train one model per category, with no separately fitted scoring components."}
